hadiths[114]{hadithnumber,text}:
"3296","The Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) would sometimes pass by a fallen date, but he would not pick it up out of concern that"
"3297","""He and 'Uthman bin 'Affan came to the Messenger of Allah to speak to him about what he had distributed of the Khumus of Hunain to Banu Hashim and Banu Al-Muttalib bin 'Abd Manaf. They said: 'O Messenger of Allah, you distributed it to our brothers; Banu Al-Muttalib bin 'Abd Manaf, and you did not give us anything, and our relationship to you in the same as theirs. 'The Messenger of Allah said to them: 'I see that Hashim and Al-Muttalib are the same."" Jubair bin Mut'im said: ""The Messenger of Allah did not allocate anything to Banu 'Abd Shams or Banu Nawfal from that Khumus, as he allocated to Banu Hashim and Banu Al-Muttalib."" (Using translation from Nasāʾī 4136)"
"3298","The poor person is not the one who goes away with one or two items of food, or with one or two morsels, or with one or two dates. Rather, the poor person is the one who does not have enough to fulfill his needs, and yet he does not go around asking people persistently; he is too shy to ask people persistently."
"3299","The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) commanded the payment of Sadaqat al-Fitr to be made before the people go out (for the Eid prayer). Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) himself used to pay it one or two days before (Eid). (Imam Ibn Hibban, may Allah have mercy on him, says:): Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) used to pay Sadaqat al-Fitr one or two days before Eid al-Fitr, and he used to begin fasting one or two days before the start of Ramadan."
"3300","the Messenger of Allah enjoined Zakatul-Fitr, one Sa of dates or one Sa of barley.Abdullah said: The people made two Mudd (equal to half of a Sa) of wheat as its equivalent. (Using translation from Ibn Mājah 1825)"
"3301","the Messenger of Allah enjoined Zakatul-Fitr at the end of Ramadan upon the people; a Sa' of dates or a Sa' of barley, upon everyone, free or slave, male or female, of the Muslims. (Using translation from Nasāʾī 2503)"
"3302","Ibn Umar reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prescribed Zakat-ul-Fitr of Ramadan one sa' of dates or one sa' of barley for every individual among the Muslims (whether) free man or slave, male or female, young or old. (Using translation from Muslim 984e)"
"3303","""The Messenger of Allah enjoined Zakatul-Fitr, a Sa' of dates or a Sa of barley, upon the free person and the slave, male and female, young and old, among the Muslims. He commanded that it be given before the people went out to the ('fd) prayer."" (Using translation from Nasāʾī 2504)"
"3304","The Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) ordered the giving of one sa’ of dates or one sa’ of barley as zakat al-fitr on behalf of every Muslim, young or old, free or slave. Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrates: After that, people considered two mudd of wheat to be equivalent to it."
"3305","""We used to pay Salaqatul Fitr when the Messenger of Allah was among us; a Sa' of food, or a Sa' of dates, or a Sa' of barley, or a Sa' of cottage cheese. We continued to do so until Mu'awiyah came from Ash-Sham and one of the things that he taught the people was when he said: I think that two Mudds of wheat from Ash-Sham are equivalent to a Sa' of this, So the people took to that."" (Using translation from Nasāʾī 2513)"
"3306","I will give Sadaqat al-Fitr in the same way as I used to give during the blessed time of the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him), that is, one sa’ of dates or one sa’ of wheat or one sa’ of barley or one sa’ of cheese. One of those present said to him: Or two mudd of wheat? He replied: No, this is the price fixed by Sayyiduna Mu'awiyah (may Allah be pleased with him); I will not accept it nor will I act upon it."
"3307","I will always give in the form of a sa', just as we used to do in the blessed time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): a sa' of dates, a sa' of barley, a sa' of raisins, or a sa' of cheese. What he meant was that this was given as Sadaqat al-Fitr."
"3308","""While we were with the Messenger of Allah in the early hours of the morning, some people came who were naked and barefoot, with their swords hung (around their necks). Most of them, may all of them, belonged to the tribe of Mudar. The face of the Messenger of Allah changed when he saw them in poverty. He went in (to his house) then he came out and ordered Bilah to call the Adhan and then the Iqamah. He (the Prophet) prayed, tjem je addressed te,, (reciting the Verses): 'O mankind! Be dutiful to your Lord, Who created you from a single person (Adam), and from him(Adam) He created his wife [Hawwa (Eve)], and from them both He created many men and women: and fear Allah through Whom you demand (your mutual right), and (do not cut the relations of) the wombs (kinship). Surely, Allah is Ever and All-Watcher over you.' [1] and: 'Fear Allah and keep your duty to Him. And let every person look to what he has sent forth for the morrow,' [2] Then they gave in charity, some giving a Dinar, others a Dirham, or a garment, or a Sa' of wheat or, a Sa' of dates, until he said: 'Even half a date.' A man from among the Ansar came with a bag of money which his hands could hardly lift. The people followed one another (in giving charity) until I saw two heaps of food and clothing, and I saw the face of the Messenger of Allah shining like gold (with joy). The Messenger of Allah said: 'Whoever sets a good precedent in Islam, he will have the reward for that, and the reward of those who acted in accordance with it, without that detracting from their reward in the slightest. And whoever sets an evil precedent in Islam, he will have a burden of sin for that, and the burden of those who acted in accordance with it, without that detracting from their burden in the slightest.""' (Using translation from Nasāʾī 2554)"
"3309","the Messenger of Allah said: ""Indeed charity extinguishes the Lord's anger and it protects against the evil death."" (Using translation from Tirmidhī 664)"
"3310","The narrator is in doubt, perhaps these are the words), ""until judgment is given among the people."" The narrator named Yazid has reported this. The narrator named Abu'l-Khair used to give something in charity every day, even if it was just a piece of bread or an onion."
"3311","The Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Whoever can save himself from Hell, he should do so, even if it is by giving half a date in charity."
"3312","A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked, ""O Messenger of Allah ﷺ! Which charity is the most superior in reward?"" He replied, ""The charity which you practice while you are healthy, niggardly and afraid of poverty and wish to become wealthy. Do not delay it to the time of approaching death and then say, 'Give so much to such and such, and so much to such and such.' And it has already belonged to such and such (as it is too late)."" (Using translation from Bukhārī 1419)"
"3313","The example of the spender and the miser is like two men who are wearing armor from their necks to their chests. When the spender intends to spend, his armor loosens and becomes spacious, until (it becomes so loose that it falls down) and reaches his feet and covers the marks of his feet. As for the miser, when he intends to spend, every ring of his armor remains firmly in its place and becomes tight, and he tries to loosen it, but it does not become loose; he tries to loosen it, but it does not become loose."
"3314","One who has the longest hands amongst you would meet me most immediately. She farther said: They (the wives of Allah's Apostle) used to measure the hands as to whose hand was the longest and it was the hand of Zainab that was the longest amongst them, as she used to work with her hand and Spend (that income) on charity. (Using translation from Muslim 2452)"
"3315","The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""The one whose hand is the longest."" The narrator states: The pure wives took a stick and began to measure their hands with it, and Lady Sawda bint Zam'a (may Allah be pleased with her) passed away first because she used to give much charity. (Lady Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, narrates) We thought that by the Prophet's (peace and blessings be upon him) statement, 'the one whose hand is the longest,' he meant the one who gives the most charity."
"3316","The Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Whoever among the Muslims gives charity from pure earnings—and Allah Almighty accepts only that which is pure—then Allah Almighty takes (that charity) in His Hand of Power and begins to nurture it, just as a person raises his young animal, until a single date becomes as large as the mountain of Uhud."
"3317","narrates this statement of the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him): ""Indeed, Allah Almighty increases for any one of you a single date or a single morsel, just as a person raises and nurtures his young animal, until that (single morsel or single date becomes like the mountain of Uhud)."
"3318","The Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""When any one of you gives charity from pure earnings—even if it is a single date—Allah Almighty accepts only pure earnings. Allah Almighty takes it in His Hand of Power and begins to nurture it, just as one of you raises his young animal, until in the Hand of Allah Almighty, it (the single date) becomes like a mountain."
"3319","Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \""Whoever gives charity from pure earnings, even if it is equal to a single date, only pure things are presented before Allah Almighty. Allah takes it in His Hand of Power, turns it over, and then increases it for the giver, just as one of you raises and nurtures his young animal, until that (single date) becomes like a mountain.\"""""""
"3320","Sayyiduna Hakim bin Hizam (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) addressed the women, giving them advice and admonition, and instructed them to fear Allah Almighty and to obey their husbands. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: Some women among you will enter Paradise. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) brought his fingers together and said: And some among you will become fuel for Hellfire. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) spread his fingers apart. Then, perhaps a woman belonging to a certain tribe asked: O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)! What is the reason for this? The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: You are ungrateful to your husbands, you curse frequently, and you are ungrateful for good."
"3321","The Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) used to go out on the days of Eid al-Fitr and Eid al-Adha. He (peace be upon him) would perform two rak'ahs, then say salam, then face the people in the Eid ground and stand. Then he (peace be upon him) would sit and face the people and say to them: Give charity and alms. So most of the women would give their earrings and rings, etc., in charity. If the Prophet (peace be upon him) had to dispatch an expedition, he would discuss it; otherwise, he would return."
"3322","The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) first performed the Eid prayer on the day of Eid, then he delivered the sermon, then he went to the women and instructed them to give charity and alms."
"3323","The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said to the women: “Give charity, for the majority of the inhabitants of Hell are from among you.” A woman who was not from a prominent family asked: “What is the reason for this?” (The narrator is unsure, perhaps these are the words:) “Why?” The Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: “You frequently curse and are ungrateful to your husbands.” Sayyiduna Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) says: “Women, despite being deficient in intellect and religion, often prevail over sensible men in matters.” It was asked: “What is the deficiency in their intellect and religion?” He replied: “As for their intellect, it is that the testimony of two women is equal to that of one man. As for the deficiency in their religion, it is that there are days in which a woman cannot perform even a single prayer.”"
"3324","the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ""Feed the hungry, visit the sick, and free the ʿānī (captive). Sufyān said: ʿānī means captive."
"3325","The Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) also proceeded towards the Eid prayer ground. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) led us in prayer, then while delivering the sermon, the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: O people! This is the day of giving charity, so give charity and alms. The narrator says: So someone took off his ring, someone took off his garment (i.e., an extra sheet). Sayyiduna Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) kept collecting them, until when the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) saw that there was no one left who had anything to give, the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) moved towards the women and said: O group of women, this is the day of giving charity, so give charity. So some woman took off her necklace, some took off her ring, some took off her anklets. Sayyiduna Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) kept collecting them, until when the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) observed that there was no one left to give, Sayyiduna Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) returned and we also returned."
"3326","The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: O Abu Dharr! I do not like that I should have gold equal to Mount Uhud, and then by the third evening, even a single dinar from it remains with me, except for that dinar which I have kept aside for repaying a debt. Regarding this gold, I would keep saying about Allah’s servants: Give this much to him, give this much to him, meaning I would keep spending it in front of me, behind me, to my right, and to my left. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: O Abu Dharr, those who possess abundance (of wealth and riches) in this world will be the ones with little (reward) on the Day of Judgment. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said to me: Remain in your place until I come to you. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) left, and then after the darkness of night had spread, he returned. I heard a sound, and I feared that perhaps some harm had come to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him). I was about to set out, but then I remembered the instruction of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), so I remained seated in my place until the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) himself came. I submitted to him: O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)! At first, I intended to come to you, but then I remembered your instruction. I had heard a sound. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: That was Jibreel (peace be upon him); he came to me and informed me that any person from my Ummah who dies in such a state that he does not associate anyone with Allah will enter Paradise. I asked: Even if he commits adultery or steals? He said: Even if he commits adultery or steals. The narrator named Jarir says: This same narration, with another chain, is also transmitted from Sayyiduna Abu Darda (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) in a similar manner. (Imam Ibn Hibban, may Allah have mercy on him, says:): There are two clarifications hidden in this narration. One of them is: Whoever dies in such a state that he does not associate anyone with Allah Almighty will enter Paradise, if Allah Almighty, by His grace, forgives the sins he committed in the world. The reason is: A person in this world inevitably commits some forbidden act, so this condition will be hidden in the narration. And the second condition is: If a person dies in such a state that he does not associate anyone with Allah, he will enter Paradise. This means: Allah Almighty will punish him in Hell for some time. We seek refuge with Allah from Hell. This will happen if Allah Almighty does not forgive him by His grace. The reason for this is so that only those people remain in Hell who associated others with Him in the world. So both these conditions are hidden in this narration. It does not mean that every person who dies in such a state that he does not associate anyone with Allah will necessarily enter Paradise."
"3327","I came to Allah's Apostle ﷺ as he was reciting:"" Abundance diverts you"" (cii. 1). He said: The son of Adam claims: My wealth, my wealth. And he (the Holy Prophet) said: O son of Adam. is there anything as your belonging except that which you consumed, which you utilised, or which you wore and then it was worn out or you gave as charity and sent it forward? (Using translation from Muslim 2958a)"
"3328","A servant says, My wealth. my wealth, but out of his wealth three things are only his: whatever he eats and makes use of or by means of which he dresses himself and it wears out or he gives as charity, and this is what he stored for himself (as a reward for the Hereafter), and what is beyond this (it is of no use to you) because you are to depart and leave it for other people. (Using translation from Muslim 2959a)"
"3329","The Noble Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, says:) When the sun sets, two angels at its sides make this announcement: O Allah! Increase the wealth of the one who spends (in Your way), and cause loss to the one who withholds.”"
"3330","""The Messenger of Allah said: 'For whom among you is the wealth of his heirs dearer to him than his own wealth?' They said: 'O Messenger of Allah, there is no one among us for whom his own wealth is not dearer to him than the wealth of his heirs.' The Messenger of Allah said: 'Know that there is no one among you for whom the wealth of his heirs is not dearer than his own wealth. Your wealth is that which you have sent on ahead, and the wealth of your heirs is that which you have kept.'"" (Using translation from Nasāʾī 3612)"
"3331","“The wealthiest will be the lowest on the Day of Resurrection, except those who do such and such with their money, and earn it from good sources.” (Using translation from Ibn Mājah 4130)"
"3332","The Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""The example of the miser and the one who gives charity is like two men wearing two iron coats of armor or two iron breastplates, which cover them from their chests up to their necks. Whenever the charitable person gives charity and intends to do so, the armor moves away from his skin, until it covers the marks of his feet and comes down to the tips of his toes (i.e., it falls down). But whenever the miser intends to spend something and thinks about it, the armor becomes tighter and each ring of it gets fixed in its place. He tries to loosen it, but it does not become loose."
"3333","narrates this statement of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): “At one of the gates of Paradise, an angel says: Who is the person who will give a loan today so that it may be repaid tomorrow? At another gate, an angel says: O Allah! Grant more to the one who spends (in Your way), and destroy the wealth of the one who does not spend.”"
"3334","The Prophet ﷺ said: A man giving a dirham as sadaqah (charity) during his life is better than giving one hundred dirhams as sadaqah (charity) at the moment of his death. (Using translation from Abū Dāʾūd 2866)"
"3335","A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked, ""O Messenger of Allah ﷺ! Which charity is the most superior in reward?"" He replied, ""The charity which you practice while you are healthy, niggardly and afraid of poverty and wish to become wealthy. Do not delay it to the time of approaching death and then say, 'Give so much to such and such, and so much to such and such.' And it has already belonged to such and such (as it is too late)."" (Using translation from Bukhārī 1419)"
"3336","The example of the person who gives charity when death approaches him is like the one who gives something after he himself is satisfied."
"3337","I have one dinar."" The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""Spend it on yourself."" He said: ""If I have another?"" The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""Spend it on your wife."" He said: ""If I have another?"" The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""Spend it on your children."" He said: ""If I have another?"" The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""Spend it on your servant."" He asked: ""If I have another?"" The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""You know best."" (Meaning, you will know yourself on whom it should be spent.)"
"3338","narrates: We used to carry loads on our backs (that is, we used to work as laborers), and then one of us would bring something and give it as charity. One person would bring half a sa’, another would bring a little more, so the hypocrites used to say: Allah is not in need of this person’s charity, and they used to say: This is just for show. So Allah the Exalted revealed this verse about them: “Those who criticize the believers who give charity willingly, and those who find nothing to give except what they earn through their hard work...”"
"3339","The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) called for him, sold him, and handed over his price to that man. Then he said: Begin with yourself, spend on yourself, then on your parents, then on your close relatives, then like this and like this."
"3340","Abu Talha was the one among the Ansar of Medina who possessed the largest property and among his property he valued most was his garden known as Bairaha' which was opposite the mosque, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ often visited it and he drank of its sweet water. When this verse was revealed: ""You will never attain righteousness till you give freely of what you love"" (iii. 91), Abu Talha got up and, going to Messenger of Allah ﷺ, said: Allah says in His Book: ""You will never attain righteousness till you give freely of what you love,"" and the dearest of my property is Bairaha' so I give it as Sadaqa to God from Whom I hope for reward for it and the treasure with Allah; so spend it, Messenger of Allah, on whatever purpose you deem it proper. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Well done! that is a profitable deal, that is a profitable deal. I have heard what you have said, but I think you should spend it on your nearest relatives. So Abu Talha distributed it among the nearest relatives and his cousins on his father's side. (Using translation from Muslim 998a)"
"3341","""We came to Al-Madinah and the Messenger of Allah was standing on the Minbar addressing the people and saying: 'The hand which gives is the upper hand. Start with those for whom you are responsible; your mother, your father, your sister, your brother, then the next closest, and the next closet.""' (Using translation from Nasāʾī 2532)"
"3342","The slave's name was Yaqub. The Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) inquired: Who will buy him? So a man from Banu Adi bought him for eight hundred dirhams. The Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) said: When a person is poor, he should begin with himself; if he has surplus wealth, he should spend on his wife; if he has surplus wealth, he should spend on his close relatives; if there is still surplus, then he should spend here and there."
"3343","Had you gives her to your maternal uncles, you would have a greater reward. (Using translation from Muslim 999)"
"3344","""Giving charity to a poor person is charity, and (giving) to a relative is two things, charity and upholding the ties of kinship."" (Using translation from Nasāʾī 2582)"
"3345","""The Messenger of Allah said: ""The best of charity is that which is given when you are self-sufficient, and start with those for whom you are responsible.""' (Using translation from Nasāʾī 2544)"
"3346","Abu Hurairah reported I asked Messenger of Allah ﷺ, What kind of sadaqah is most excellent? He replied What a man with little property can afford to give; and begin with those for whom you are responsible. (Using translation from Abū Dāʾūd 1677)"
"3347","""The Messenger of Allah said: 'A Dirham was better than a hundred thousand Dirhams.' They said: 'O Messenger of Allah, how?' He said: 'A man had two Dirhams and gave one in charity, and another man went to part of his wealth and took out a hundred thousand Dirhams and gave them in charity.""' (Using translation from Nasāʾī 2528)"
"3348","""I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, what charity is best?' He said: 'Giving water to drink.'"" (Using translation from Ibn Mājah 3684)"
"3349","The Prophet ﷺ said: ""There are three whom Allah (SWT) loves: A man who comes to some people and asks (to be given something) for the sake of Allah and not for the sake of their relationship, but they do not give him, so a man stayed behind and gave it to him in secret, and no one knew of his giving except Allah (SWT) and the one to whom he gave it. People who travel all night until sleep becomes dearer to them than anything equated with it, so they lay down their heads (and slept), then a man among them got up and started praying to Me and beseeching Me, reciting My Verses. And a man who was on a campaign and met the enemy and they fled, but he went forward (pursuing them) until he was killed or victory was granted."" (Using translation from Nasāʾī 1615)"
"3350","The second is the person who is on an expedition, and the people scatter, so he says takbeer and plunges into the fight until Allah grants him victory or he is martyred. The third is the person who is traveling at night with some people, and when the night becomes deep, they camp at a place, and at that time sleep is dearest to them, so they sleep, and that person stands up and recites verses and supplicates in My presence. And Allah does not like an old adulterer, a miser, or an arrogant person."" The narrator also mentioned the third person."
"3351","The Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""The poor person is not the one who goes away with one or two dates or with one or two morsels. The poor person is the one who does not possess that which suffices his needs, and his needy state is not known so that charity may be given to him."" By ""deprived"" is meant this person (who is mentioned in the Qur'an)."
"3352","""The poor man (Miskin) is not the one who goes around asking people and they send him away with a morsel or two, of a date or two. ""They said: ""Then what does poor (Mishkin) mean?"" He said: ""The one who does not possess independence of means and no one notices him to give charity to him, and he does not stand and ask of people."" (Using translation from Nasāʾī 2572)"
"3353","""My mother died unexpectedly; if she had been able to speak she would have given charity. Should I give charity on her behalf?"" The Messenger of Allah said: ""Yes."" So he gave charity on her behalf. (Using translation from Nasāʾī 3649)"
"3354","""Sa'd bin 'Ubadah went out with the Prophet on one of his campaigns, and death came to his mother in Al-Madinah. It was said to her (as she was dying): 'Make a will.' She said: 'To whom shall I make a will? The wealth belongs to Sa'd.' Then she died before Sa'd came. When Sa'd came, he was told about that and he said: 'O Messenger of Allah, will it benefit her if I give in charity on her behalf?' The Prophet said: 'Yes.' Sa'd said: 'Such and such a garden is given in charity on her behalf' -regarding a garden that he named."" (Using translation from Nasāʾī 3650)"
"3355","While a person was in the wilderness he heard a voice from the cloud (commanding it thus): Irrigate the garden of so and so. (After that the clouds slinked aside and poured water on a stony ground. It filled a channel amongst the channels of that land and that person followed that water and he found a person standing in the garden busy in changing the course of water with the help of a hatchet. He said to him: Servant of Allah, what is your name? he said: So and so. And it was that very name which he had heard from the clouds. and he said to him: Servant of Allah, why do you ask me my name? He said: I heard a voice from the clouds of which is the downpour, saying: Water the garden of so and so, like your name. What do you do (for the favour) shown to you by Allah in this matter? He said: Now as you state so. I look what yield I get from it, and I give one-third as charity out of it and I and my children eat one-third of it and one-third I return to it as investment. (Using translation from Muslim 2984a)"
"3356","A man expressed his intention to give charity, so he came out with charity and placed it in the hand of an adulteress. In the morning, the people were talking and saying: charity was given to an adulteress last night. He (the giver of Sadaqa) said: 0 Allah, to Thee be the praise-to an adulteress. He then again expressed his intention to give charity; so he went out with the charity and placed it in the hand of a rich person. In the morning the people were talking and saying: Charity was given to a rich person. He (the giver of charity) said: 0 Allah, to Thee be the praise-to a well-to-do person. He then expressed his intention to give charity, so he went out with charity and placed it in the hand of a thief. In the morning, the people were talking and saying: Charity was given to a thief. So (one of the persons) said: 0 Allah, to Thee be the praise (what a misfortune it is that charity has been given to) the adulteress, to a rich person. to a thief! There came (the angel to him) and he was told: Your charity has been accepted. As for the adulteress (the charity might become the means) whereby she might restrain herself from fornication. The rich man might perhaps learn a lesson and spend from what Allah has given him, and the thief might thereby refrain from committing theft. (Using translation from Muslim 1022)"
"3357","Apostle of Allah, I have nothing with me, but only, that which is given to me by Zubair (for household expenses). Is there any sin for me if I spend out of that which is given to me (by Zabair)? Upon this he (the Holy Prophet) said: Spend according to your means; and do not hoard, for Allah will withhold from you. (Using translation from Muslim 1029d)"
"3358","The Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""When a woman gives charity from her husband's house, without causing any harm, that woman will receive its reward, her husband will receive its reward, the man will receive the reward for earning it, and the woman will receive the reward for her intention, and the treasurer's example is also similar to this."
"3359","The honest Muslim trustee who spends (sometimes he said"" who gives"" ) what he is commanded to do and he gives that in full with his heart overflowing with cheerfulness and he gives it to one to whom he is ordered, he is one of the givers of charity. (Using translation from Muslim 1023)"
"3360","The narrator named Muhammad bin Zaid is Muhammad bin Zaid bin Muhajir bin Qanfar Jad‘ani Qurashi. He heard hadith from Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) and Sayyiduna Muawiyah bin Abu Sufyan (may Allah be pleased with him), while Imam Malik and the people of Madinah have transmitted narrations from him."
"3361","The Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""The upper hand is better than the lower hand."
"3362","The Prophet ﷺ said: Hands are of three types: Allah's hand is the upper one; the bestower's hand is the one near it; the beggar's hand is the lower one. So bestow what is surplus, and do not submit yourself to the demand of your soul. (Using translation from Abū Dāʾūd 1649)"
"3363","The best charity is that which is given when one is in a state of prosperity (or after giving which a person remains prosperous), and the upper hand is better than the lower hand, and each of you should begin with those under his care, because his wife will say, 'Spend on me,' his children will say, 'To whom are you handing me over?' his slave will say, 'Give me food, then take work from me.'"" (Imam Ibn Hibban, may Allah have mercy on him, says:): The statement of the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), ""The upper hand is better than the lower hand,"" in my view, means: the hand of the one who gives charity is superior to the hand of the one who asks. It does not mean that the one who takes without asking is superior, because it is impossible that the hand for which an action has been declared permissible becomes inferior to another person who has been commanded to perform a certain act as an obligation and he fulfills it, or that the other person seeks nearness to Allah Almighty through a voluntary act. Sometimes, the giver adopts fewer means in his giving compared to the one for whom taking has been made permissible. And sometimes, the one for whom taking has been made permissible is more virtuous and more pious than the one who is giving. So, when it is impossible to apply this without declaring one superior to the other, it becomes established that the one who gives charity is superior to the one who asks for it."
"3364","the Messenger of Allah said, when mentioning charity and those who refrain from asking. ""The upper hand is better than the lower hand; the upper hand is that which gives and the lower hand is that which asks."" (Using translation from Nasāʾī 2533)"
"3365","I know beforehand."" So the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) said to her: ""Do not count, otherwise Allah the Exalted will also give to you by counting."
"3366","I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) say: ""Allah does not accept prayer without ablution, nor does He accept charity given from unlawful wealth, and you have been the governor of Basra (from where you had income)."
"3367","The Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Whoever amasses wealth unlawfully and then gives it in charity, he will have no reward for it; its burden will be upon him."
"3368","Who has planted these trees of dates-a Muslim or a non-Musim? She said: A Muslim, of course, whereupon he said: Never a Muslim plants, or cultivates a land, and it out of that men eat, or the animals eat, or anything else eats, but that becomes charity on his (planter's) behalf. (Using translation from Muslim 1552b)"
"3369","I heard the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) say: ""Whenever a Muslim plants a garden, and any beast or bird eats anything from it, that person receives its reward."
"3370","The Prophet (peace be upon him) had actually set out in search of the (Quraysh) caravan, and the Quraysh had set out to protect their caravan. Without any prior arrangement, both armies confronted each other, just as Allah Almighty has stated: And by my life! Among the battles of the Prophet (peace be upon him), the most honorable in the eyes of the people is Badr, but I do not wish that I had participated in it in exchange for the honor of having pledged allegiance on the night of Aqabah, for we had pledg"
"3371","I want to leave the area where I committed the sin and I want to move towards you and live with you. I present all my wealth as charity in the court of Allah and His Messenger (peace be upon him) and I disassociate myself from it. So the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) said: Giving one third of it (in charity) is permissible for you (or will be sufficient for you)."
"3372","The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) turned away from him. He came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) from another side and said the same thing, but the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) again turned away from him. Then he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) from the front, so the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) took it from him and struck it in such a way that if it had hit him, it would have wounded or hurt him. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: One of you brings all his wealth to give it in charity, and then sits down to beg from people. Charity is that which is given while one is in a state of ease. Take your wealth back from us; we have no need of it."
"3373","She took the oath of allegiance to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said to him: Messenger of Allah, a poor man stands at my door, but I find nothing to give him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to her: If you do not find anything to give him, put something in his hand, even though it should be a burnt hoof. (Using translation from Abū Dāʾūd 1667)"
"3374","Sayyida Umm Bujayd Ansariyyah (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates that the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Return the beggar (by giving him something), even if it is a burnt hoof."" (Imam Ibn Hibban, may Allah have mercy on him, says:): The statement of the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), ""Return the beggar,"" is a literal command, but the real intent is to prohibit (refusing). What he means is: Return the beggar by giving him something, even if it is a burnt hoof."
"3375","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) narrates: The Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \""Whoever asks you in the name of Allah, give him something, and whoever seeks refuge in the name of Allah, grant him refuge, and whoever invites you, accept his invitation.\"""""""
"3376","narrates: We used to carry loads, then when someone would bring something to give in charity, it would be said: He has done this for show. If someone brought half a sa’, it would be said: Allah is independent of this. So regarding this, the verse was revealed: ""Those who criticize the believers concerning the charities they give."
"3377","The Prophet replied: ""There are many doors of goodness: saying « » , saying «» , saying « » , reciting « » , enjoining good, forbidding evil, removing something harmful from the path, making something clear to a deaf person, guiding a blind person, guiding someone who asks for direction regarding a matter, striving to help a distressed person due to the strength of your legs, lifting a burden for a weak person due to the strength of your arms — all these are charity from you for your own self."
"3378","Your prophet (May peace be upon him) said : Every good act is a SADAQAH (almsgiving). (Using translation from Abū Dāʾūd 4947)"
"3379","The Prophet ﷺ said, Enjoining, all that is good is a Sadaqa."" (Using translation from Bukhārī 6021)"
"3380","The Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) said: Allah Almighty has created three hundred and sixty joints in every human being, so whoever, in the number of three hundred and sixty, does (the following deeds): recites « » , recites «» , recites « » , recites « » , recites «» , removes a bone from the people's path, removes a stone from their path, enjoins good and forbids evil (and does all these deeds three hundred and sixty times), then by the evening he will be in such a state that he has saved himself from Hell."
"3381","The Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Charity is due upon every joint of a person on every day the sun rises (i.e., it is obligatory to do so daily). Reconciling between two people with justice, helping a man to mount his animal, or helping him load his belongings onto it, or lifting his goods for him, and removing something harmful from the road (all these actions) are acts of charity."
"3382","The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah knows best. So he informed: (Allah Almighty has said: When I am mentioned, you (peace be upon you) will also be mentioned along with Me.)"
"3383","The Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""A child born as a result of adultery, a person who reminds others of his favors, a disobedient (to parents), and a habitual drinker of alcohol will not enter Paradise."" (Imam Ibn Hibban, may Allah have mercy on him, says:): The Prophet Muhammad's (peace and blessings be upon him) negation of the entry into Paradise for a child born as a result of adultery, even though such a child should not bear the burden of his parents' sin, means: Children born as a result of adultery generally commit more prohibited acts. Therefore, by this, the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) meant that a child born as a result of adultery will not enter that part of Paradise where those people will enter who were not born as a result of adultery and did not frequently commit prohibited acts."
"3384","The Prophet [SAW] said: ""No one who reminds others of his favors, no one who is disobedient to his parents and no drunkard, will enter Paradise."" (Using translation from Nasāʾī 5672)"
"3385","The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his companions: ""Will you not pledge allegiance to me?"" The people said: ""O Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him)! We have already pledged allegiance to you once. Upon what should we pledge allegiance to you (again)?"" The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""You should pledge allegiance to me that you will not associate anything with Allah, you will establish prayer, and you will pay zakat."" After that, the Prophet (peace be upon him) said in a low voice: ""And that you will not ask anything from people."" (Imam Ibn Hibban, may Allah have mercy on him, says:): The statement of the Prophet (peace be upon him), ""That you will not associate anything with Allah,"" by this he meant to command not to commit shirk. Similarly, his statement, ""That you will not ask anything from people,"" by this he meant to command not to ask (from people)."
"3386","The Prophet ﷺ said: Acts of begging are lacerations with which a man disfigures his face, so he who wishes may preserve his self-respect, and he who wishes may abandon it; but this does not apply to one who begs from a ruler, or in a situation which makes it necessary. (Using translation from Abū Dāʾūd 1639)"
"3387","narrates that the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Whenever a person opens the door of begging for himself, Allah opens the door of poverty upon him. If a person takes a rope, gathers a bundle of wood, carries it on his back, goes to the market and sells it, and eats from it, this is better for him than asking people, whether they give him something or not."
"3388","narrates that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Allah is pleased with three things for you and dislikes three things for you. He is pleased for you that you worship Him and do not associate anything with Him, and that you all hold fast to the rope of Allah, and that you all give sincere advice to the one whom Allah has appointed over your affairs. And He dislikes for you idle talk, wasting wealth, and asking too much."
"3389","Do not press in a matter, for I swear by Allah, none of you who asks me for anything and manages to get his request while I disdain it, will he be blessed in that which I give him. (Using translation from Muslim 1038a)"
"3390","The narrator says: I thought, my she-camel Yaqutah is better than one uqiyah. The narrator states: One uqiyah is equal to forty dirhams."
"3391","The Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Whoever asks from people in order to increase his wealth, then he is collecting embers of Hell; now let him collect little or let him collect much, as he wishes."
"3392","I give it to him and he goes away, even though he has only carried fire in his garment."
"3393","the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Whoever begs from people so as to accumulate more riches, he is asking for alive coal from hell, so let him ask for a lot or a little.” (Using translation from Ibn Mājah 1838)"
"3394","I take a document about which I do not know what is written in it? This would be like a document that causes misunderstanding. Sayyiduna Muawiyah (may Allah be pleased with him) informed the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) about what both of them had said. The Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) went out for some task. In the early part of the day, the Prophet (peace be upon him) passed by a camel that was made to sit outside the door of the mosque. Then, in the latter part of the day, the Prophet (peace be upon him) passed by it again, and it was still in the same place. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: Where is its owner? He was searched for, but not found. So the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: Fear Allah regarding these animals; ride them when they are healthy, and feed them well so that they become fat and strong. Then the Prophet (peace be upon him), expressing displeasure over the earlier incident, said: Whoever asks for something while he possesses that which makes him not in need of asking, then he is only increasing the coals of Hell for himself. The people asked: O Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him)! What is meant by not being in need of asking? The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: Whoever has food for the morning and evening. (Imam Ibn Hibban, may Allah have mercy on him, says:) The statement of the Prophet (peace be upon him), “what he does in the morning and what he does in the evening,” means what he always does, until he becomes independent of it. Have you not seen that the Prophet (peace be upon him) said in the narration transmitted from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him): “It is not permissible to give charity to a wealthy person or to a healthy, working person.” So the Prophet (peace be upon him) here set the limit for the prohibition of charity as that which makes a person independent of people. You certainly know that whoever has food for the morning and evening is not among those who become independent of others to the extent that it becomes forbidden for him to take charity. On this basis, the wording of these narrations has been transmitted in a general sense. But what is meant is obligatory charity, not voluntary charity."
"3395","Sayyiduna Qabeesah bin Mukhaariq (may Allah be pleased with him) when some members of his tribe sought his help regarding t"
"3396","Sayyiduna Qabeesah bin Mukhaariq Hilaali (may Allah be pleased with him): I took responsibility for a payment, so I presented myself befo"
"3397","The Prophet ﷺ said: Acts of begging are lacerations with which a man disfigures his face, so he who wishes may preserve his self-respect, and he who wishes may abandon it; but this does not apply to one who begs from a ruler, or in a situation which makes it necessary. (Using translation from Abū Dāʾūd 1639)"
"3398","I went to the service of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the intention that I would ask him for something. I heard him saying during the sermon: ""Whoever wants to remain independent, Allah will keep him independent; whoever refrains from asking, Allah will protect him from asking; and whoever asks us for something, we will give it to him."" Sayyiduna Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: I returned and did not ask the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for anything, and today among the Ansar, I have the most wealth."
"3399","I have, I will give it to you."
"3400","Some of the Ansar begged from the Messenger of Allah (May peace be upon him) and he gave them something. They later begged from him again and he gave them something so that what he had was exhausted. He then said :What I have I shall never store away from you but Allah will strengthen the abstinence of him who abstains, will give a satisfaction to him who wants to be satisfied, and will strengthen the endurance of him who shows endurance. No one has been given a more ample gift than endurance. (Using translation from Abū Dāʾūd 1644)"
"3401","Be cautious about ahadith except those which were current during the reign of Umar, for he exhorted people to fear Allah, the Exalted and mMajestic. I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as saying: He upon whom Allah intends to bestow goodness, He confers upon him an insight in religion; and I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as saying: I am the treasurer. To one whom I give out of (my own) sweet will, he would be blessed in that, but he whom I give (yielding to) his constant begging and for his covetousness is like one who would eat, but would not be satisfied. (Using translation from Muslim 1037a)"
"3402","""I asked the Messenger of Allah and he gave me, then I asked him and he gave me. The he said: 'O Hakim, this wealth is attractive and sweet. Whoever takes it without being greedy, it will be blessed for him, and whoever takes it with avarice, it will be blessed for him. He is like one who eats and is not satisfied. And the upper hand is better than the lower hand.' I said: 'O Messenger of Allah! By the One Who sent you with the truth, I will never ask anyone for anything after you, until I depart this world. ""' (Using translation from Nasāʾī 2603)"
"3403","Qabeesah bin Dhuwaiyb (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: Sayyiduna Umar bin Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) gave Ibn Sa'di two thousand dinars, but he refused to take them and said: I have no need for this. Sayyiduna Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: I am going to tell you the same thing that the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me. ""When Allah Almighty gives you some provision without you asking for it, without you being greedy for it, then take it, because Allah Almighty has granted it to you."
"3404","Abu Ya'la informed us: Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi narrated to us: al-Muqri' narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Abi Ayyub narrated to us: Abu al-Aswad narrated to me: from Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj: from Busr ibn Sa'id: from Khalid ibn 'Adi al-Juhani (may Allah be pleased with him): he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) say: ""Whoever receives a gift from his brother without asking or coveting, let him accept it and not refuse it, for it is provision that Allah has sent to him."" Abu Hatim (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""This command which we have been ordered to implement is accepting what one is given. The two known conditions that make it permissible in their absence are asking and coveting. If one of them is found in a wealthy person who is independent with what he possesses, he is prevented from accepting what he is given, unlike the needy poor. The situation in which it is permissible for a person to accept what he is given, even if asking and coveting are present, is a state of necessity. Necessity is of two types: necessity due to having property and necessity due to lack. Necessity due to having property is when a person owns a large amount of the chattel of this world besides food and drink, and he is in a place where food and drink are not sold at all. So even if he is possessor, his ruling is that of a person in need; he may accept what he is given, even if he is asking or coveting. Necessity due to lack is clear and does not need explanation."""""
"3405","ʿUmar bin al Khattab ؓ appointed me in charge of the zakah, and when had finished with it and handed it over to him, he ordered that I be given some remuneration. I said: I only did it for the sake of Allah and my reward is with Allah. He said: Take what is given to you. I was appointed to do some work at the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he gave me some remuneration, and I said the same as you have said, but the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: ʿIf you are given something without asking for it, then take it and give some in charity.” (Using translation from Aḥmad 371)"
"3406","""I asked the Messenger of Allah and he gave me, then I asked him and he gave me, then I asked him and he gave me. Then he said: ""This wealth is attractive and sweet. Whoever takes it without insisting, it will be blessed for him, and whoever takes it with avarice, it will not be blessed for him. He is like one who eats and is not satisfied. And the upper hand is better than the lower hand.""' (Using translation from Nasāʾī 2531)"
"3407","I heard Sayyiduna Abul Qasim, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: ""Indeed, he does not truly thank Allah who does not thank the people."
"3408","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrates: The Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: \""Whoever seeks refuge with you in the name of Allah, grant him refuge; and whoever asks you in the name of Allah, give him something; and whoever invites you, accept his invitation; and whoever does good to you, repay him; and if you do not find anything with which to repay him, then pray for him so much that you feel you have repaid him.\"" (Imam Ibn Hibban, may"
"3409","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) narrates: The Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \""Whoever asks you in the name of Allah, give to him; whoever seeks refuge in the name of Allah, grant him refuge; and whoever invites you, accept his invitation.\"""""""
